# Copy to .env.test.local (gitignored) to enable the integration suites.
#
# EVERYTHING here must point at a DEDICATED, DISPOSABLE test environment.
# NEVER the shared/production project: the suites create users, send emails
# and consume MFA verifications — against the real project a test run can
# lock actual editors out (15 MFA verifies/hour per IP, shared by the whole
# office; 2 emails/hour on the built-in sender).
#
# RECOMMENDED TARGET: the LOCAL Supabase stack (no cloud project needed).
#   npx supabase start          # Docker required; config in supabase/config.toml
#   npm run db:migrate          # with DIRECT_URL below, applies the schema
# Local perks: emails land in Mailpit (http://127.0.0.1:54324), never sent;
# rate limits are raised in config.toml; `npx supabase db reset` wipes clean.
# The values below are the CLI's standard local defaults — paste the keys
# `supabase start` prints (they are demo keys, identical on every machine).
#
# Without this file (or without SUPABASE_TEST_PROJECT="true") every
# integration suite skips; unit tests always run.

# Explicit declaration that this target is disposable. The suites refuse to
# run without it.
SUPABASE_TEST_PROJECT="true"

# Local stack Postgres (session mode; port 54322). Runtime and migrations can
# share it — Supavisor transaction-mode quirks don't apply locally.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres"

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:54321"
# From `npx supabase start` output:
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="<anon key from supabase start>"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="<service_role key from supabase start>"

PREVIEW_TOKEN_SECRET="any-test-secret"
